INT. THE ROYAL OAK FUNCTION ROOM - NIGHT

Bunting, half-fallen. A disco ball turning slowly over an empty dance floor. Somewhere, a speaker plays something by Take That, very quietly, as if embarrassed.

Outside, rain on the car park. The sort that can't be bothered to stop.

TIM HARGREAVES (40s, tie round his head like a headband, pint in each hand) sits on a stack of chairs. Opposite him, TOM HARGRAVE (40s, immaculate, one button undone, a medal in a velvet box on his knee).

A WAITER wheels a trolley of cold sausage rolls between them. Neither takes one.

A WEDDING GUEST'S DOG, a spaniel in a bow tie, sleeps under a table.

TIM
Lovely do.

TOM
Mm.

TIM
Lovely speech, as well. The bit about the cricket.

TOM
I wasn't talking about cricket.

TIM
Weren't you? I thought you were.

Quiet. The disco ball turns.

HATTIE (30s, bridesmaid, shoes in hand) drifts over, a plate of trifle balanced on her wrist.

HATTIE
Right, you two. Dad says you're to stop sulking by the buffet.

TOM
We're not sulking, Hattie.

TIM
We're discussing.

HATTIE
You're discussing a medal in a box, Tim. In the corner. At my sister's wedding.

TIM
It's Tom's medal.

TOM
It's our medal, strictly speaking.

HATTIE
Of course it is.

She eats a spoonful of trifle. Looks from one to the other.

HATTIE (CONT'D)
Tom. Tim. You were twenty-two. It was a relay. It was nineteen years ago.

TIM
Nineteen and a half.

TOM
And I'd have won if someone had passed the baton.

TIM
I passed the baton.

TOM
You threw the baton.

A pause. The waiter reverses his trolley, very slowly, out of range.

TIM
(quietly)
I tripped, Tom.

TOM
You tripped on purpose.

TIM
Don't be absurd.

TOM
You told Gillian I'd cheated at the trials. She told the committee.

TIM
That was a joke!

TOM
Nineteen years, Tim. Nineteen years, and every Christmas, it's a joke.

The spaniel wakes. Lifts its head.

HATTIE
Boys, please. My sister is cutting the cake in an hour.

TIM
(standing)
I am not having this, Tom. Not here.

TOM
(standing)
Then where?

Beat. Tim sets down both pints. Tom puts the medal on the chair.

The disco ball catches the light.

TIM
Outside.

TOM
Outside.

They go. The doors swing shut behind them. A single sausage roll rolls off the trolley and lands on the floor.

HATTIE
(to the spaniel)
Right. Well. That's the evening.

Rain. A shout, muffled. A crash of metal bins.

The spaniel puts its head back down.
